    "title": "Projected Super El Niño on the way. What it could mean for Carolinas",
    "text": "CAROLINAS (QUEEN CITY NEWS) — An El Niño is expected later this year and is projected to be very strong, with some referring to it as a “Super El Niño.” An El Niño is when water temperatures in the central and eastern equatorial Pacific are warmer than normal. This is because westerly trade winds are weaker, allowing warmer water to flow away from Asia and Australia, and then push back across towards the central and eastern equatorial Pacific. Some prediction models show this El Niño could be one of the strongest in a while, with water temperatures possibly 2.0 degrees Celsius above normal. These climate phenomena can change weather patterns across the globe and here at home. During an El Niño, the southern branch and/or subtropical jet stream become amplified, allowing for more stormy weather across the southern U.S. While the polar jet stream lifts further north, producing less stormy weather across the northern U.S. More stormy weather across the southern U.S. also allows for cooler temperatures, while warmer-than-normal weather fills into the northern U.S. Hurricane seasons in the Atlantic and Pacific basins can be affected by El Niño events due to changes in wind shear. In the Atlantic basin, wind shear tends to increase during El Niño years, leading to less tropical activity. The opposite happens in the Pacific basin, creating more tropical systems there due to less wind shear. This is not always the case. In 2023, during an El Niño year, the Atlantic basin had 19 named storms, which was the fourth-most active hurricane season at the time. 2023 was also the year Major Hurricane Idalia made landfall in the Big Bend area of Florida as a category three storm. Remember, even with less tropical activity projected for the upcoming hurricane season, it only takes one landfalling hurricane for an active season. Warmer-than-normal ocean water temperatures can also counteract some of the windshear impacts from El Niño. For the Carolinas, an El Niño tends to lead to drier and warmer summers, with wetter and cooler winters. The big reason for the active winter is that the more amplified southern branch of the jet stream can lead to the development of more nor’easters. Impacts for the upcoming El Niño may take months to be realized, but it is something to monitor for later this year.",
